From f1d2d9fc79074509fb965db398c009893be4b2eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: william arzt <bill@tlon.io>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:45:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] urwasm: cut per-call overhead of the stateful jet

A stateful call (%gent/%rand) reloads the saved instance, runs, and saves
it again. Four parts of that round trip cost far more than the call:

- The ~20 Nock kicks that build the per-context match data (batteries and
  contexts of the Lia arrows) ran on every call. Memoize them in the
  persistent memo cache, keyed by the core context. The memo is bypassed
  inside +mice, where the persistent cache is harvested and returned to
  Hoon.

- Every function in the module was recompiled on every call. Compile
  lazily instead (wasm3 already compiles on first call via op_Compile).
  Lazily compiled code is not laid out reproducibly, and a suspended
  computation stores positions in compiled code, so: a state that is
  already suspended is loaded eagerly as before, and a call that ends
  suspended after a lazy load is discarded and redone eagerly from the
  same saved state (held by reference, so a nested run on the same seed
  cannot tombstone it away). Modules seen to suspend are remembered (by
  mug, as a performance hint only). In lazy mode the code arena spills
  into extra chunks instead of being moved and retried, since code may
  be running in it.

- Saving linear memory scanned it for trailing zeros a byte at a time.
  Drop whole trailing zero pages with memcmp first.

- The struct arena was saved at its full capacity (8 MB), zeroed, copied
  and measured on every call. Save only the bytes in use.

Measured on aarch64 macOS, ReleaseFast, per do-nothing call: 0.96 ms ->
0.12 ms for a tiny module, 5.49 ms -> 0.22 ms for a 540 KB module,
17.3 ms -> 2.2 ms with 64 MB of zeroed memory. tests/lib/wasm passes
unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
